	LispView Interface for the XView Textsw Module 


Most of this interface is only a thinly disguised LispView version 
if the XView Textsw (textedit sub window) package.  In particular most of 
the Textsw attributes only differ in name, e.g. TEXTSW_AUTO_INDENT
becomes an accessor called textedit-auto-indent in LispView.
A list of the textedit attibutes that are identical for LispView
and XView, there are about 25, appears near the end of this document.

[Note (31-oct-91): You cannot use the LispView textedit-window menu
to save the current file; see "BUGS" below.]

TEXTEDIT-LOCATION                                               [Type]

A textedit-location represents this position of a character as the 
offset of the character from the beginning of the buffer.  The value
of textedit-location must be one of :first, :last, or a positive integer.


initialize-instance ((x TEXTEDIT-MARK)                           [Method]
  &key


initialize-instance ((x TEXTEDIT-WINDOW)                         [Method]
  &key
    contents
  &allow-other-keys

The value of contents can be a pathname or a string.  
Initargs that correspond to all of the textedit attribtues 
listed in the table at the end of this section are also supported.  
The names of the initargs are the same as the accessor without 
the "textedit-" prefix.


READ-ONLY ((x textedit-window))                                  [Method]

Returns nil if the contents of the textedit-window can be edited
by the user.  Change with setf.


TEXTEDIT-CONTENTS (x textedit-window)                            [Method]
   &key
     start
     end

Return the contents of the textedit-window between :start and :end 
as string.


(SETF TEXTEDIT-CONTENTS) value (x textedit-window)               [Method]
   &key 
     insertion-point

Value must be a string or a pathname.  The string or the contents of 
the file specified by pathname are inserted begining at the specified
insertion-point which defaults to the current insertion point.


TEXTEDIT-CONTENTS-LENGTH (x textedit-window)                     [Method]

Returns the number of characters in the textedit-windows contents.



TEXTEDIT-INSERTION-POINT ((x textedit-window))                   [Method]

Returns the current insertion-point, a textedit-location, or changes 
it with setf.   


TEXTEDIT-INSERT ((x textedit-window) value)                      [Method]

Value must be a string or a pathname.  The string or the contents of
the file are inserted at the current insertion point.


TEXTEDIT-DELETE ((x textedit-window) start end                   [Method]
   &key update-clipboard 

Remove the text between textedit-locations start and end.  If 
update-clipboard is non nil then the deleted text is copied to the 
clipboard.  The default value for update-clipboard is nil.


TEXTEDIT-VISIBLE-TEXT-OFFSET (x textedit-window)                 [Method]
   &optional unit

Multiple-value returns the first and last visible line numbers if unit
is :line, or character locations is unit is :char.  The default value of unit 
is :char.  The first visible line or character can be changed with setf.


TEXTEDIT-MODIFIED-P ((x textedit-window))                        [Method]

Returns non nil if there are any outstanding edits for this file.


TEXTEDIT-FILENAME ((x textedit-window))                          [Method]

If the contents of the textedit-window are backed up by a file, e.g. 
because they were loaded from a file, then return the files name.
The value of textedit-filename can be changed with setf.


TEXTEDIT-INSERT-MAKES-VISIBLE ((x textedit-window))	         [Method]

Value must be one of :always :if-auto-scroll.


TEXTEDIT-LINE-BREAK-ACTION ((x textedit-window))                 [Method]

Value must be one of :clip :wrap-at-char :wrap-at-word.



TEXTEDIT-SAVE ((x textedit-window)                               [Method]
   &key 
     filename
     notice-x
     notice-y

Save the contents of the textedit-window.  If no filename was specified,
the textedit-windows contents didn't come from a file, and no file has
ever been associated with this textedit-window via (setf textedit-filename),
then prompt the user with a notice.  The upper left hand corner of the
notice will appear at :notice-x and :notice-y relative to the 
textedit-window, :notice-x and :notice-y default to zero.  Textedit-save
returns the filename the contents of the textedit-window were saved to
if successful and nil otherwise.


TEXTEDIT-RESET ((x textedit-window)                               [Method]
   &key 
     notice-x
     notice-y

Discard the contents of the textedit-window.



TEXTEDIT-FIND-STRING ((x textedit-window) string                  [Method]
   &key 
     start 	
     end
     from-end

Seach the contents of the textedit-window from :start to :end (or, if 
the value of :from-end is non nil from :end to :start) for the 
first occurence of string.  If an exact match is found the position 
of the leftmost element of the match is returned unless :from-end 
is non nil, then the rightmost element of the match is returned.


The table below identifies all of the LispView textedit-window accessors
that map one to one XView textsw attribute.  The semantics of the LispView 
accessors are the same as for XView; the O'Reilly "XView Programming Manual" 
should be used as a reference.  The accessors for attributes whose type is 
boolean are non standard: the reader always returns NIL or T even if the
attribute has been set to a non nil value other than T.  This was done
to avoid the overhead required to cache Lisp values for each boolean attribute.

LispView Accessor                 XView Attribute                 Type                                
-----------------                 ---------------                 ----                                
textedit-left-margin              XV_LEFT_MARGIN                  (integer 0 *)                       
textedit-right-margin             XV_RIGHT_MARGIN                 (integer 0 *)                       
textedit-bottom-margin            XV_BOTTOM_MARGIN                (integer 0 *)                       
textedit-top-margin               XV_TOP_MARGIN                   (integer 0 *)                       
textedit-margin                   XV_MARGIN                       (integer 0 *)                       
textedit-font                     XV_FONT                         font                                
textedit-again-recording          TEXTSW_AGAIN_RECORDING          boolean                             
textedit-auto-indent              TEXTSW_AUTO_INDENT              boolean                             
textedit-auto-scroll-by           TEXTSW_AUTO_SCROLL_BY           (integer 0 *)                       
textedit-blink-caret              TEXTSW_BLINK_CARET              boolean                             
textedit-browsing                 TEXTSW_BROWSING                 boolean                             
textedit-checkpoint-frequency     TEXTSW_CHECKPOINT_FREQUENCY     (integer 0 *)                       
textedit-confirm-overwrite        TEXTSW_CONFIRM_OVERWRITE        boolean                             
textedit-control-chars-use-font   TEXTSW_CONTROL_CHARS_USE_FONT   boolean                             
textedit-disable-cd               TEXTSW_DISABLE_CD               boolean                             
textedit-disable-load             TEXTSW_DISABLE_LOAD             boolean                             
textedit-history-limit            TEXTSW_HISTORY_LIMIT            (integer 0 *)                       
textedit-ignore-limit             TEXTSW_IGNORE_LIMIT             (integer 0 *)                       
textedit-insert-makes-visible     TEXTSW_INSERT_MAKES_VISIBLE     (member :always :if-auto-scroll)    
textedit-line-break-action        TEXTSW_LINE_BREAK_ACTION        (member :wrap-at-char :wrap-at-word)
textedit-lower-context            TEXTSW_LOWER_CONTEXT            (integer -1 *)                      
textedit-memory-maximum           TEXTSW_MEMORY_MAXIMUM           (integer 0 *)                       
textedit-multi-click-space        TEXTSW_MULTI_CLICK_SPACE        (integer 0 *)                       
textedit-multi-click-timeout      TEXTSW_MULTI_CLICK_TIMEOUT      (integer 0 *)                       
textedit-store-changes-file       TEXTSW_STORE_CHANGES_FILE       boolean                             
textedit-submenu-edit             TEXTSW_SUBMENU_EDIT             menu                                
textedit-submenu-file             TEXTSW_SUBMENU_FILE             menu                                
textedit-submenu-find             TEXTSW_SUBMENU_FIND             menu                                
textedit-submenu-view             TEXTSW_SUBMENU_VIEW             menu                                
textedit-upper-context            TEXTSW_UPPER_CONTEXT            (integer -1 *)                      
textedit-read-only                TEXTSW_READ_ONLY                boolean                             


BUGS:

- *** Attempt to save current file from LispView textedit-window menu
      causes signal stack error
"Save Current File" (from the "File" submenu) does NOT work;
attempting to use it causes a stack pointer error which effectively ends
the Lisp session.  "Store as New File..." has the same problem in the
case where the specified "new" file is the current file.
The current file can be saved by invoking (lv:textedit-save ew),
where ew is the textedit-window.

- Accessors for the textedit-submenus attributes have not been implemented 
although the corresponding initargs should work.


- The  (textedit-line-break-action (member :wrap-at-char :wrap-at-word))
accessor fails - XView bug #1047539


- The interface for Textsw notifications, e.g. CAPS_LOCK, hasn't been finished.
It's of dubious value anyway.


- visible-text-offset fails if viewing the end of a file, (setf visible-text-offset)
fails if value is :last


- No support for the following Textsw features has been included:

   Setting the primary selection.

   Dealing with multiple views.

